      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 26680.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 729,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning each water molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the literal foundation of life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The flowing water we drink, swim in, and use to irrigate crops.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and polar ice caps.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, and steam rising from boiling pots.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unusual scientific properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its electrical polarity (one side is slightly positive, the other slightly negative), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients through our bodies and how oceans stay salty.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant redwood trees against gravity).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different; it expands and becomes *less* dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a glass, and crucially, why lakes freeze from the top down rather than the bottom up, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a massive amount of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and prevents living organisms from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite Earth being covered in water, **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use (in lakes, rivers, and shallow aquifers).\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nBiology cannot exist without water:\n*   **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs 83%, and even our bones are about 31% water. \n*   **Metabolism:** Water is the medium in which all the chemical reactions of life take place. Without it, cells cannot transport energy, get rid of waste, or maintain their structure.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the landscapes of our planet, drives our weather and climate, and flows through the cells of every living creature on Earth."
